{
	"id": "effect-promise-fix",
	"text": "To fix this, we would have to wrap our Promise in a thunk. Then each time we call it, a fresh Promise is created. Alas, it is too easy to make meaningful and likely erroneous semantic changes to your programs. You have to be extremely careful when refactoring Promise -strewn code. Instead, let's call Effect.promise, wrapping up our Promise thunk into a perfectly cozy and inert Effect. When this is run, the thunk will be called, which will kick off a new Promise, and the Effect will succeed when the underlying Promise resolves.",
	"code": "const sensorData: Promise<any> =\n  fetch(\"/api/sensors\").then(r => r.json())",
